# The `gst:Provision` in force on one invoice's own date — a direct lookup,
# not a detector. `amount-mismatch.sparql` derives the identical "latest
# effectiveFrom on or before the date" answer as one step inside a larger
# comparison; this exists because an agent investigating a single invoice
# needs that same answer on its own, addressable by name with the invoice
# as the only input (Epic 105 P106 Slice 4a,
# `plans/106-agent-trace-hygiene.md`) — before this, the only way to reach
# it was `query_graph` free-hand SPARQL, rediscovering the same
# `OPTIONAL` + `!BOUND` idiom `amount-mismatch.sparql`'s own comment
# already explains once.
#
# **`{{invoice}}` is the one caller-supplied value, and it is bound through
# `VALUES`, never concatenated into the query text.** The substitution that
# fills it in (`Catalog::run_pack_query`) only ever replaces the text
# between the double braces, and only after checking it is a bare,
# syntax-safe IRI — so the worst a caller can do is name an invoice that
# does not exist, never rewrite the query itself.
#
# **"Latest in force" is `OPTIONAL` + `!BOUND`, not `FILTER NOT EXISTS`**,
# for the identical reason `amount-mismatch.sparql`'s own comment gives:
# the pushdown planner does not descend into `NOT EXISTS`, so the
# provisions would never be loaded and the guard would be trivially true
# for every one of them.

PREFIX gst: <https://graph-owl.dev/packs/gst#>

SELECT ?provision ?capPercent ?citation ?effectiveFrom
WHERE {
  GRAPH ?register {
    VALUES ?invoice { <{{invoice}}> }
    ?invoice gst:invoiceDate ?date .
  }

  GRAPH ?law {
    ?provision a gst:Provision ;
               gst:effectiveFrom ?effectiveFrom ;
               gst:capPercent    ?capPercent ;
               gst:citation      ?citation .
  }
  FILTER (?effectiveFrom <= ?date)

  OPTIONAL {
    GRAPH ?laterLaw {
      ?later a gst:Provision ;
             gst:effectiveFrom ?laterFrom .
    }
    FILTER (?laterFrom <= ?date && ?laterFrom > ?effectiveFrom)
  }
  FILTER (!BOUND(?later))
}
